ow does the the value of our money play  into inflation because you know if the  price of everything is going up  naturally of course the value of the  current money we have is going down is  that right yeah the the buying power is  reducing right dollars are strengthening  in this climate they're not weakening so  dollar devaluation is a little bit  separate to inflation we're seeing  inflation here from a number of  different areas domestic policy here in  the united states i mentioned before  money printing on a huge scale being  continued by the biden administration i  mean the the deficit is 700 billion  dollars this year so printing money  flooding the economy with money fuels  inflation then we have the political  side of things right the war in russia  and ukraine fueling rising  energy costs across europe and of course  food costs across the globe so these  factors are contributing and fueling the  domestic inflation we're seeing here in  the united states and of course this is  a global issue now as well what about uh  since we're defining terms you know the  other one that's become controversial is  is recession itself  uh and so we're we there's there's  warring factions saying whether we're in  a recession or not or what it even means  it seems like in washington they've kind  of i guess it's kind of convenient they  can just redefine the term whatever they  want uh depending on what they need so  so  are we in one and can you give us a  definitive definition of  what what it actually means  of course yeah  look it's very clear what a recession  means the definition has been in place  for decades two consecutive quarters of  negative gdp growth which is exactly  what we've seen for the last two  quarters the biden administration  obviously recession doesn't suit their  political goals so they're attempting to  change the definition it is two quarters  of negative gdp growth that's what it is  we are in a technical recession there's  no other way to look at it  so we can't rely  on  washington dc to  save us here  so what what do you think from uh from  from just a you know in everyday  american  household  uh they have no direct control over  monetary policy so what can they do  to protect themselves in this uh  environment  well you're right look you know the the  big picture is very hard to control  right  as you mentioned our control is very  limited we can go out and vote and push  for change so the trajectory longer term  can move and i think that's important  because with the biden administration's  appetite to spend it's going to be very  difficult to get these problems under  control they're making the federal  reserve's job very difficult  but  that's longer term change i think what  we all need to do is sort of narrow the  scope look at those around us and say  how can we protect  and i think precious metals what we deal  with here at birch very conducive for  climates like this it's a very tough  climate for investors people who are  looking at retirement because the  options are limited stocks historically  have been a very good hedge on inflation  today they're not right stock market's  down about 20 percent for the year so  far so stocks are adding to the problem  of inflation because your money is  coming down at the same time as  everything is becoming more expensive  cash bonds  not working today interest rates are so  low inflation is higher than the rate of  interest on most bonds so people are  losing buying power sitting in bonds or  cash  that's where precious metals come in  they're safe haven assets but more  importantly they're very conducive for  climates like this specifically when  stock markets crash safe haven demand  goes up gold and silver tend to go up  they doubled in value within 18 months  of the last crash on top of that  inflation drives them up remember the  definition i gave before inflation is  the rising cost of commodities and  services over time gold and silver are  commodities so by definition they  increase as the cost of living does so i  think everybody needs to be thinking  about a hedge how can i protect a  portion of my portfolio against the  current economic issues and like i said  precious metals about the best way to do  that in climates like this now how do  you compare  something like gold or silver to to  something like bitcoin we're hearing  that that's a  digital gold we're hearing that's  another hedge against inflation last  time i saw the price though it was it  was a falling so for people who are  hearing that what would you say to them  look gold is ingrained it's been used  for centuries it's used by central  governments today to support currency  it's got a long track record we haven't  seen that with cryptocurrency in fact  you know for a long time cryptocurrency  was moving in lockstep with the nasdaq  so it wasn't providing a hedge as you  rightly point out as inflation has been  skyrocketing here in the united states  crypto has been coming down so it  doesn't have the established  relationship with other markets that we  see with gold so i'm not sure it's a  hedge what it is is a speculative asset  it grew very well for investors now it's  going the other way  are cryptocurrencies are fad  i would say not i think currency broadly  longer term it's a natural progression  for currency to move digital  but there's a lot of question marks will  government move in to that space i think  at some point it makes sense that they  will if that happens what happens to all  of these third-party currencies right  does the government step in and say hey  we're going to start taxing these things  so there's a lot of question marks there  i personally see it as a speculative  asset and gold i see on the opposite end  of the spectrum right you're not going  to put money in gold and quadruple it in  a year we've seen that with crypto but  at the same time it doesn't tend to drop  in the same way either so opposite ends  of the spectrum in many ways  uh phil we know you have a heart out but  i guess my my my last question is uh  well second to last is  well how do you explain so why why is uh  why are precious metals so stable in the  long term how do you explain the  stability of it what makes them so safe  it's just how they've been used if you  look at gold specifically gold is a  store of value and buying power and it  has always been that as an example if  you went back to biblical times one  ounce of gold would buy somebody 400  loafs of bread  today at four dollars and some change  one ounce of gold buys 400 loaves of  bread  so gold's buying power has always  remained consistent which is why  countries use it to support currency  it's actually currency that fluctuates  right as dollars become weaker over time  it takes more weaker dollars to buy that  ounce of gold hence that inverse  relationship between the two gold has  always been a store of wealth it's  finite right it's divisible  that's why it's used as currency so it's  just always been that way and it and you  know for the foreseeable future it  always will  well last question and uh i have to ask  you this because this is just a rule for  everyone that comes in the studio even  even even digitally uh i need to know  do you believe in space aliens are there  aliens in the world  listen  i have to say there are so many planets  there are so many i mean it has to be  somewhere there has to be life on a  planet somewhere  my my knowledge here is very limited but  that's my very basic basic view that was  the correct answer i'm so thankful for  that well phil thank you so much for  joining us on the show if you want to  learn more about precious metals and uh  investing in a stable asset like that  text walsh to 989898  for a free info kit on diversifying into  gold that's walsh to  98989 philip thanks again we appreciate  it  thank you so much for having me  if you want to join my exclusive fan  base or a cult as really 